+The opt-in `control_messages` microbenchmark measures the production
+`wire::write_message` path into a preallocated, reused memory sink:
+
+```powershell
+cargo bench --bench control_messages --features bench-internals -- --case plan-short-256 --iterations 10000 --repeats 5
+```
+
+Cases are `plan-short-1`, `plan-short-256`, `plan-short-4096`, their `plan-long-*`
+counterparts with long UTF-8 paths, `commit` (36-byte body), and `noop`.
+Every plan includes symlink descriptors. Descriptors and sink capacity are prepared
+before timing; packet length, roundtrip, and final bytes are checked outside it.
+The `[control-perf]` rows report whole-loop wall/CPU/cycle counts and `iterations`;
+divide counts by iterations for per-message values. `entries` and `bytes` are
+totals across the loop; `entries_per_message`, `symlinks_per_message`, packet/body
+sizes, and fingerprint describe one message. CPU covers the process during the
+loop; RSS is lifetime peak. This is serialization plus memory-sink writes, not
+network latency. The no-op loop reports overhead without automatic subtraction.
+Use a separate `bench-allocations` binary with `--allocations` to count allocations;
+uninstrumented `bench-internals` binaries provide timing comparisons. Match case,
+iterations, fingerprint, instrumentation and source/compiler/binary provenance.
 
